    {
      "id": "wxRe4B3uPQVfndCZeKoMB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -6368.112820030271,
      "y": -2205.3843018207367,
      "width": 1573.181640625,
      "height": 3896.1999999999994,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b00",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 159404542,
      "version": 5259,
      "versionNonce": 696777322,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786706396356,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Angle 1)\n\nIntegrated Iterable in prod at ImmoScout24\n    -> I know the product/API side & release quality\n            concerns @ marketing automation\nNot personally led a full Salesforce Marketing Cloud to Iterable migration end to end\n    -> Closest transferable proof is RWE: legacy Java to NestJS modernization, \n        done in slices, with tests and production-risk control\n\nFor this project, I'd start by mapping journeys, templates, events and deliverability risk\nin the first two weeks before commiting to a global plan.\n\nQs) Timeline pressure ?\n\nQ Agenda:\n1. Why migration matters now ? What are the deadlines ?\n\n0)\n1. In SFMC, segmentation logic does not live in the Journey Builder but in the SQLs from Automation Studio.\nIterable does not have an equivalent for this\n\n2. In iterable, type of project and id key are set permanently and cannot be changes once projecct created.\n\n3. From the 600 emails, aprox. how many of them did actively send messages in the last 12 months ?\n\n1)\n\nSFMC | Iterable | Migration concern\n\nSubscriber Key / Contact Key | userId or email | irreversible\nData Extension  (sendable)  | User Profile + Lists/Segments | Iterable does not have arbitrary tables\nData Extension  (non-sendable) | Catalog, event data or Data Feed\nContact Builder + data rel. | User profile + events | Iterable does not do joins. Data comes denormalized\nAutomation studio + SQL Query Activity | no eqv.\nJourney Builder Journey | Journey | not 1:1\nAMPscript / SSJS / GTL | Handlebars\nLookup() / LookupRows() în AMPscript | no eqv. -> profile / Catalog / Data Feed\n\n< Strategy: make them talk 70% of the call >\n\nAngle 2) \n\n1. 60s opener\n\nI'm a senior backend developer w. Node and TypeScript. \n\nMy last engagement was at Bitpanda, working on insitutional crypto custody for banks. \n\nI've integrated Iterable in production at ImmoScout24 - a React/Node platform on AWS. \n\nI've led legacy migrations, the biggest at RWE - migrating Java monolith to NestJS.\n \nFrom what Razvan sent over, the 600 emails block stood out - but tell me first - where did this whole project come from ?\n\n2. Discovery Qs\n\n    1. What's making you migrate now ? Is it a contract expiring, cost, or a technical limit you've hit ?\n\n    2. Have you attempted this before ? If so, what happened ?\n\n    3. How many journeys and automations are live right now ? How many templates in total ?\n\n    4. How much of the segmentation sits in SQL queries in the Automation Studio, versus the Journey Builder itself?\n\n    5. Of the aprox. 600 emails, how many actually sent anything in the last 12 months ?\n\n    6. Where does the consent for users to receive marketing news live ? In what form ? (SFMC, CRM, other tools?)\n        Not know ? follow up Q: Who would know that ?\n        * If it's in SFMC, we'd need to get it under Iterable cause GDPR consent is linked to SFMC *\n\n    7. What's your monthly send volume, how many contacts/users ?\n\n    8. Who does marketing operations on your side, and do they have capacity for this ? \n\n    9. Deadline ? Budget is allocated ? \n\n3. Honest Positioning\n\nNo, I haven't led a full migration off SFMC. I have integrated It. in prod at ImmoScout24, so I know the data model\nand API firsthand\n\nAnd I have led legacy migrations - at RWE, Java to NestJS. sliced and covered by tests rather than rewritten in one go.\n\n\nThe 600 email block is a refactoring and data modeling problem, which is exactly what I do.\n\n\nLet me tell you how I'd approach it and what I'd want to check in the first two weeks.\n\n4. Rate & Duration\n\n\n\"600 could be 8 templates or 80, and that's several times the effort. I won't give you a number until I've seen 20 or 30 samples.\"\n\n5. The Three Pitfalls\n\n1 - Where does logic live ? ( real logic isn't in the Journey Builder )\n\nHow much of your segmentation actually lives in the scheduled SQL query activities rather than the Journey Builder itself ?\nThat's usually where most of the logic sits and Iterable has no equivalent -  so every query becomes either a segment, if data\nis already into the user profile, or a job on your side that computes the field and pushes back in. This part would also touch \nthe engineering team, not just marketing.\n\n< If they ask \"and how much is that?\": \"depends how many queries there are and what data they depend on\n — that's the first list I'd ask for.\" >\n\n2. Consent & Unsubscribe\n\nThis isn't a mapping. It's a decision. SFMC has opt-out at three levels: global, list, publication list - plus send classification.\nIterable has a globall channel and message type. They only partially overlap.\nThe rule I'd apply is: where it's ambigous, the most restrictive interpretation wins. \nWith GDPR, getting this wrong isn't a bug but legal exposure.\n\n3. Deliverability & Warm up\n\nA new sending domain and a new IP mean warm up, and reputation does not transfer between ESPs. \nThere can't be a big bang. You go campaign by campaign, in parallel, starting with the most engaged segments.\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Angle 1)\n\nIntegrated Iterable in prod at ImmoScout24\n    -> I know the product/API side & release quality\n            concerns @ marketing automation\nNot personally led a full Salesforce Marketing Cloud to Iterable migration end to end\n    -> Closest transferable proof is RWE: legacy Java to NestJS modernization, \n        done in slices, with tests and production-risk control\n\nFor this project, I'd start by mapping journeys, templates, events and deliverability risk\nin the first two weeks before commiting to a global plan.\n\nQs) Timeline pressure ?\n\nQ Agenda:\n1. Why migration matters now ? What are the deadlines ?\n\n0)\n1. In SFMC, segmentation logic does not live in the Journey Builder but in the SQLs from Automation Studio.\nIterable does not have an equivalent for this\n\n2. In iterable, type of project and id key are set permanently and cannot be changes once projecct created.\n\n3. From the 600 emails, aprox. how many of them did actively send messages in the last 12 months ?\n\n1)\n\nSFMC | Iterable | Migration concern\n\nSubscriber Key / Contact Key | userId or email | irreversible\nData Extension  (sendable)  | User Profile + Lists/Segments | Iterable does not have arbitrary tables\nData Extension  (non-sendable) | Catalog, event data or Data Feed\nContact Builder + data rel. | User profile + events | Iterable does not do joins. Data comes denormalized\nAutomation studio + SQL Query Activity | no eqv.\nJourney Builder Journey | Journey | not 1:1\nAMPscript / SSJS / GTL | Handlebars\nLookup() / LookupRows() în AMPscript | no eqv. -> profile / Catalog / Data Feed\n\n< Strategy: make them talk 70% of the call >\n\nAngle 2) \n\n1. 60s opener\n\nI'm a senior backend developer w. Node and TypeScript. \n\nMy last engagement was at Bitpanda, working on insitutional crypto custody for banks. \n\nI've integrated Iterable in production at ImmoScout24 - a React/Node platform on AWS. \n\nI've led legacy migrations, the biggest at RWE - migrating Java monolith to NestJS.\n \nFrom what Razvan sent over, the 600 emails block stood out - but tell me first - where did this whole project come from ?\n\n2. Discovery Qs\n\n    1. What's making you migrate now ? Is it a contract expiring, cost, or a technical limit you've hit ?\n\n    2. Have you attempted this before ? If so, what happened ?\n\n    3. How many journeys and automations are live right now ? How many templates in total ?\n\n    4. How much of the segmentation sits in SQL queries in the Automation Studio, versus the Journey Builder itself?\n\n    5. Of the aprox. 600 emails, how many actually sent anything in the last 12 months ?\n\n    6. Where does the consent for users to receive marketing news live ? In what form ? (SFMC, CRM, other tools?)\n        Not know ? follow up Q: Who would know that ?\n        * If it's in SFMC, we'd need to get it under Iterable cause GDPR consent is linked to SFMC *\n\n    7. What's your monthly send volume, how many contacts/users ?\n\n    8. Who does marketing operations on your side, and do they have capacity for this ? \n\n    9. Deadline ? Budget is allocated ? \n\n3. Honest Positioning\n\nNo, I haven't led a full migration off SFMC. I have integrated It. in prod at ImmoScout24, so I know the data model\nand API firsthand\n\nAnd I have led legacy migrations - at RWE, Java to NestJS. sliced and covered by tests rather than rewritten in one go.\n\n\nThe 600 email block is a refactoring and data modeling problem, which is exactly what I do.\n\n\nLet me tell you how I'd approach it and what I'd want to check in the first two weeks.\n\n4. Rate & Duration\n\n\n\"600 could be 8 templates or 80, and that's several times the effort. I won't give you a number until I've seen 20 or 30 samples.\"\n\n5. The Three Pitfalls\n\n1 - Where does logic live ? ( real logic isn't in the Journey Builder )\n\nHow much of your segmentation actually lives in the scheduled SQL query activities rather than the Journey Builder itself ?\nThat's usually where most of the logic sits and Iterable has no equivalent -  so every query becomes either a segment, if data\nis already into the user profile, or a job on your side that computes the field and pushes back in. This part would also touch \nthe engineering team, not just marketing.\n\n< If they ask \"and how much is that?\": \"depends how many queries there are and what data they depend on\n — that's the first list I'd ask for.\" >\n\n2. Consent & Unsubscribe\n\nThis isn't a mapping. It's a decision. SFMC has opt-out at three levels: global, list, publication list - plus send classification.\nIterable has a globall channel and message type. They only partially overlap.\nThe rule I'd apply is: where it's ambigous, the most restrictive interpretation wins. \nWith GDPR, getting this wrong isn't a bug but legal exposure.\n\n3. Deliverability & Warm up\n\nA new sending domain and a new IP mean warm up, and reputation does not transfer between ESPs. \nThere can't be a big bang. You go campaign by campaign, in parallel, starting with the most engaged segments.\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15
    },
